decision\": \"revert\", \"reason\": \"Intrinsic hypothesis DISPROVEN: still uniform RUNTIME_ERROR with exp-based tanh, and inspection of the installed Triton (.venv/.../triton/language/__init__.py exports 'sigmoid') shows tl.sigmoid is in fact available -- so sigmoid was never the issue. Four candidates now fail identically with no traceback exposed by the evaluator (feedback.json/log carry only status=RUNTIME_ERROR, sol_ms=null => run() raises during first invocation). Since I cannot execute Triton/CUDA/python here to read the error, the next candidate c005 will INSTRUMENT run(): wrap the kernel launch in try/except that writes the full traceback to a file inside the task workspace (docs/diag_c005.txt) and then RE-RAISES (candidate still legitimately fails, no computational fallback). This surfaces the actual compile/runtime error text for precise fixing.\", \"cumulative_evals\": 4, \"skills_used\": [], \"tokens_note\": \"eval 4 of 100 budget\"}","+{\"candidate\": \"c005\", \"parent\": \"c004\", \"source_hash\": \"3ae122ae1e35627dcf1ece208802a8bd719f50e3b34b18764ef785e51ce611c1\", \"design\": \"DIAGNOSTIC candidate: kernel body byte-identical to c004; only run() changed to wrap the launch in try/except that appends traceback.format_exc() to docs/diag_c005.txt (inside the task workspace) and RE-RAISES the original exception. No fallback, no result fabrication, exception not swallowed -> candidate still legitimately fails.\", \"hypothesis\": \"Persisting the traceback will reveal the true compile/runtime cause hidden behind the evaluator's opaque RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"change_from_parent\": \"added try/except+traceback-to-file+re-raise around the launch; no math/config change\", \"validation\": {\"analytic_checks\": \"kernel math unchanged from c004\", \"shape_dtype_audit\": \"unchanged\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"fcd64c92-9a71-592e-ab33-71843be9270f\", \"B\": 64, \"S\": 613, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"pass\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"5834489e-1f51-5995-b499-8765bd31a705\", \"B\": 64, \"S\": 128, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"pass\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"f2508211-757f-5c74-a08a-621ce070f77d\", \"B\": 4, \"S\": 256, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"pass\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"e9c4303d-4391-5cb3-a6e1-674c1d19bbfd\", \"B\": 64, \"S\": 256, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"pass\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"a6c812e5-eb06-5abe-a527-84583b7e08ba\", \"B\": 8, \"S\": 373, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"pass\": false, \"speedup\": null}], \"geomean_speedup\": null, \"all_pass\": false, \"decision\": \"revert\", \"reason\": \"SUCCESS as a diagnostic: docs/diag_c005.txt captured the true cause for all 5 shapes -> triton.compiler.errors.CompilationError NotImplementedError('only tuple comprehensions are supported') at the line `pcw = [[tl.load(...) for q in range(3)] for p in range(9)]`. This Triton build (evaluator venv, py3.12) does NOT support Python LIST comprehensions inside @triton.jit; only TUPLE comprehensions. Every candidate c001-c005 used list comprehensions / list literals of triton values, so all failed identically at compile. Fix for c006: rewrite the kernel with ZERO list comprehensions and no list literals holding triton values -- fully unroll N=3 with explicit named scalars (and use tuple(...) or explicit loops where iteration is needed). Also revert the diagnostic try/except so run() is clean. Interpreter path confirms python3.12 build.\", \"cumulative_evals\": 5, \"skills_used\": [], \"tokens_note\": \"eval 5 of 100 budget; diagnostic paid off - root cause is list-comprehension unsupported\"}"," "," ","+"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
