{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-acd3b16b-2059-4fe0-8d6f-fc162fc0f753","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"Claude-Opus-4.8","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The solution is confirmed at the c012 best source (hash `7f7be732…`), and I've now systematically exhausted the tuning space. Assessing convergence:\n\n**Full knob map — every dimension explored, all peak at c012 (12.0211×):**\n- `BLOCK_ROWS`: {8→7.55, 16→**8.08**, 32→7.47}@nw16; {8→10.55, 16→**12.02**}@nw8\n- `num_warps`: {16→8.08, 8→**12.02**, 4→7.19}@BR16\n- loop pipelining (`num_stages=2`): 11.65 < 12.02\n\nThe two most recent candidates (c014, c015) both regressed. The kernel is the intended fully-fused, single-pass, read-once, row-blocked design sitting at the memory-bound optimum. The only untried idea (exact H-tiling to cut the 44% masked lanes) is high-risk/low-value: masked loads are predicated off so they don't touch the memory bottleneck, and the multi-pass tiling alternative was already measured inferior (c006 tiled 4.3× < c007 single-pass 4.6×), while single-pass exact width is impossible since `tl.arange` requires a power of two.\n\nThis meets the \"genuinely converged, no justified next candidate\" condition. Writing the completion marker (no evaluation this turn)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":259842,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":4871,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e9806933-2899-41b5-90ed-01d25aa07f47","uuid":"5f2dda3d-07af-4b58-a81f-3c73f97d7739","timestamp":"2026-09-17T21:10:37.013Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-acd3b16b-2059-4fe0-8d6f-fc162fc0f753","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"Claude-Opus-4.8","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CqWFS9deo3Ynvipv6WpKC6","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-040_altup_predict_correction_cycle_backward/SEARCH_COMPLETE","content":"# SEARCH_COMPLETE\n\nTask: L2/040 `altup_predict_correction_cycle_backward` (SOL-ExecBench, A800 sm_80)\n\n## Outcome\nBest valid candidate: **c012** — geometric-mean speedup **12.0211×** over the\nreference, **5/5 feedback workloads PASSED** (per-workload 8.41–14.04×).\n`solution/solution.py` is left byte-identical to the c012 source\n(sha256 `7f7be73208934c404291f33f1bad5e8d9d62645dbfa577e8e1e37cff72493b67`).\n\nPer-workload (c012):\n- fcd64c92 (B64,S613): 14.0395×\n- 5834489e (B64,S128): 13.5573×\n- f2508211 (B4,S256):   8.4082×\n- e9c4303d (B64,S256): 13.6200×\n- a6c812e5 (B8,S373):  11.5164×\nAll within tolerance (max_abs 0.03125, max_rel ~0.0078; a single bf16 ULP).\n\n## Winning design\nSingle fused Triton kernel implementing the whole AltUp predict→correct backward:\n- **Design B row-blocking**: grid = `cdiv(rows, BLOCK_ROWS)`, `BLOCK_ROWS=16`,\n  each program owns 16 consecutive rows.\n- **Single-pass, fully H-resident** per row: `BLOCK_H=next_pow2(2304)=4096`\n  masked; every heavy tensor (`hidden[3]`, `grad_corrected[3]`, `activated`,\n  `norm_weight`, `router_weight[3]`) read exactly once.\n- **Shared-weight amortization**: `router_weight`/`norm_weight` + the 27+9 coef\n  scalars loaded once per program.\n- **Local accumulation + single atomic flush** of `grad_router[3,H]`,\n  `grad_norm[H]`, `grad_pcw[9,3]`, `grad_ccw[3,3]` per program (~16× fewer atomics).\n- `num_warps=8`; all math fp32, `bf16` I/O; exp-based `_tanh`; exact `1/2304`.\n- **No list comprehensions / list literals of Triton values** (the build rejects\n  them) — everything unrolled into named scalars.\n\n## Why the search is complete (genuine convergence)\nThe algorithm is the intended memory-bound optimum (fused, read-once,\nrow-blocked). All three tuning dimensions were swept and every neighbor of c012\nregresses:\n- `BLOCK_ROWS`: {8:7.55, 16:8.08, 32:7.47}@nw16 ; {8:10.55, **16:12.02**}@nw8\n- `num_warps`: {16:8.08, **8:12.02**, 4:7.19}@BR16\n- loop pipelining (`tl.range num_stages=2`): 11.65 (< 12.02)\n\nThe two most recent perf candidates (c014 BR=8, c015 pipelining) both regressed.\nThe only remaining idea — exact H-tiling to remove the ~44% masked lanes — is\nhigh-risk/low-value: masked loads are predicated off and do not touch the memory\nbottleneck; the multi-pass tiled alternative was already measured slower\n(c006 tiled 4.3× < c007 single-pass 4.6×); and a single-pass exact-2304 width is\nimpossible because `tl.arange` requires a power-of-two length. No justified next\ncandidate remains.\n\n## Evaluation ledger\n14 counted evaluations (c001–c015; c010 invalidated by a foreign GPU process and\nnot counted; c011 re-ran that config). Trajectory of valid candidates:\nc006 4.3151× → c007 4.6099× (single-pass) → c008 7.5515× (row-block BR8) →\nc009 8.0782× (BR16) → **c012 12.0211× (num_warps=8)** = best.\nc001–c005 failed to compile; root cause (Triton build rejects list comprehensions\nin `@triton.jit`) was found via the c005 traceback-instrumentation diagnostic.\n\n## Notes / caveats\n- Constraints honored throughout: Triton-only (no Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension\n  fallback), evaluated only via `scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback`,\n  append-only `candidates.jsonl`, immutable candidate IDs, no `final` run.\n- `altup_active_idx` handled generally via `if IDX==…` on the constexpr (feedback\n  fixes it to 0; the hidden 16-workload set may differ).\n- `final` (full 16-workload) evaluation is operator-only and has NOT been run;\n  awaiting explicit operator approval to run `final c012`.\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_to